  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nA weak cold front has sagged into our region this afternoon.\nThis front extends from an area of low pressure near the Great\nLakes. The front is expected to slowly push south through the\nregion through the remainder of this afternoon and evening. \nShowers and thunderstorms are possible along the front, but \nsubsidence on the backside of a shortwave trough moving across \nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley is suppressing much of this \npotential. That said, portions of far eastern Nebraska and \nwestern Iowa have a low chance of severe weather should a \nthunderstorm be able to develop. The main concern will be \ndamaging winds and hail if any thunderstorms become severe. \nOtherwise, much of the area will be sunny with high temperatures\nin the upper 80s to near 90. While it may be sunny, skies may \nappear milky as wildfire smoke from the Pacific Northwest is \nbeing carried over the region. Little to no surface impact is \nexpected from this smoke however.\n\nThe cold front will begin to lift northwards with the aid of a\nlow-level jet Saturday morning. At the same time, a shortwave\nimpulse will quickly move across the north-central Plains.\nConvective-allowing ensembles continue to show showers and \nthunderstorms developing over central Nebraska early in the \nmorning. These may form into a thunderstorm complex that moves \nacross eastern Nebraska and southwest Iowa later in the morning \nthrough the early afternoon. It remains uncertain if this \ncomplex becomes severe and how long it persists into our region.\nThat said, if a complex does form, damaging winds and hail \nwould be the primary concerns with this. Clouds and any \nprecipitation from the complex should keep temperatures a touch\ncooler during Saturday afternoon. Heading into late Saturday \nnight and Sunday morning, another low-level jet will impinge on \nthe area. Showers and thunderstorms will again be possible, \nmainly across western Iowa. Severe weather may be possible with\ndamaging winds and hail once again. Wildfire smoke will \ncontinue to waft over the area on Saturday.\n\nHeading through Sunday, temperatures increase slightly as the\nridge over the southwest builds slightly. Temperatures are\nlikely to push into the low 90s as a result. This will be\nshort-lived though as a shortwave rounding the ridge pushes into\nthe area Sunday night into Monday morning. These repeated\ntroughs in the northwest flow aloft continue into next week and\nwill keep at least weak surface troughing across the region \nwith active weather anticipated. Multiple rounds of \nthunderstorms is likely heading through next week as this \npattern generally holds, though overall troughing over the Great\nLakes increases. Wildfire smoke will continue to waft over the \nregion. Rain chances are largely focused overnight into the \nmorning hours in association with the low-level jet. Severe \nweather may continue to be possible as moderate winds aloft and \nsufficient instability should support at least some threat of \norganized severe convection. With the repeated thunderstorms, \ntemperatures gradually dip through the 80s early next week. \nTemperatures may even reach the 70s for highs late in the week.",
